# Writer column after a refused first-block marker

## Root cause

An indented list opener followed by a refused first-block `+` leaves its next block in the outer item. At the outer item's content column, the block attaches to the nested item. The writer invariant requires a spelling that preserves the outer item's block, but the current writer rules do not select a column among valid spellings.

## Change

Specify that the writer moves the block from the outer item's content column to the nearest column that parses it in that item. For the ruled example, this is document column 3. Keep the block on the following line without adding a separator. Add a focused test for quote and fence followers.

## Scope

The rule selects a column by parsed ownership and distance from the usual content column. It applies to any block kind and any indentation where the same ownership conflict occurs, rather than naming the two corpus rows.
